>>> how to copy multi-line from clipboard to ollama in zsh shell on macos sonoma
To copy text from the clipboard and paste it into a command line session like Ollama in a Zsh shell on macOS Sonoma, you can use the 
`pbpaste` command. Here's how you can do it:

1. Open your terminal or any command-line interface where you want to run the command.
2. To copy text from the clipboard to your terminal, you can use the following command:
   ```
   pbcopy <(pbpaste)
   ```
   This command uses `pbpaste` to read text from the clipboard and pipes it into `pbcopy`, effectively copying the content of the clipboard 
to itself.

3. Now, you can type the Ollama command followed by the paste command to copy the contents:
   ```
   ollama <(pbpaste)
   ```
   This will send the copied clipboard contents as input to the Ollama program.

If you want to use a specific text editor like Sublime Text or Atom, you can set up their respective commands to paste from the clipboard. 
Here are examples for Sublime Text and Atom:

For Sublime Text:
```
subl -n --new-window <(pbpaste)
```

For Atom:
```
atom -n <(pbpaste)
```

These commands open a new instance of the specified editor with the pasted text.

Please note that these instructions are general and should work on macOS Sonoma. If you encounter any issues, make sure your terminal 
emulator supports the `pbcopy` and `pbpaste` commands, which are part of the built-in pasteboard system on macOS.

